{"type":"route","meta":{"status":200,"headers":{"cache-control":"public, max-age=3600","content-type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/llms.txt/layout,_N_T_/llms.txt/route,_N_T_/llms.txt"}},"body":"# Amantrika\n\n> Amantrika turns an Indian wedding invitation into a single shareable link: a themed invitation website with RSVPs, directions, a countdown and a photo gallery.\n\n## What it is\n\nA host builds an invitation through a guided form, chooses a visual theme, and shares one\nlink. Guests open it on a phone, read the invitation, RSVP, and get directions to the venue.\nIt replaces the printed card and does what a card cannot: it updates when the venue changes,\ncollects RSVPs automatically, shows a countdown, and holds the photo gallery afterwards.\n\nBuilt for Indian celebrations — Hindu-tradition weddings first, with engagements, receptions,\nbaby showers, birthdays, anniversaries and griha pravesh on the same foundation.\n\n## Facts\n\n- Audience: couples and families planning an Indian celebration, and the wedding planners,\n  printers and photographers who build invitations on their behalf.\n- Market: India, plus the NRI diaspora.\n- Every invitation is one URL. The link never changes when the invitation is updated or paid for.\n- Pricing is not final and is deliberately not listed here. See the pricing page for current numbers.\n\n## Pages\n\n- [Home](http://localhost:3000/): what Amantrika is and the themes on offer.\n- [About Amantrika](http://localhost:3000/about): Amantrika began with a box of wedding cards kept for thirty years. Our story, what we believe, and what we promise the families who trust us.\n- [Changelog](http://localhost:3000/changelog): What has shipped in Amantrika, newest first — every entry is something you can use today, not something we plan to build.\n- [Contact us](http://localhost:3000/contact): How to reach Amantrika about an invitation you are building, a partnership, or anything that is not working the way it should.\n- [How Amantrika works](http://localhost:3000/how-it-works): From building your invitation to sharing the link and collecting RSVPs — what happens at each step, and what your guests see.\n- [Roadmap](http://localhost:3000/roadmap): What we are building next for Amantrika, roughly in order — written as intentions rather than promises, with dates deliberately left off.\n- [Blog](http://localhost:3000/blog): guides and traditions for Indian weddings.\n\n## Blog\n\nCategories: Guides, Inspiration, Traditions, Planning.\n\n- [Sharing your wedding invitation on WhatsApp without it getting lost](http://localhost:3000/blog/sharing-your-wedding-invitation-on-whatsapp) — What to write around the link, when to send it, and why a bare forwarded URL gets ignored by exactly the guests you most want to reach. (2026-08-02)\n- [Choosing a wedding invitation theme that still looks right in six months](http://localhost:3000/blog/choosing-a-wedding-invitation-theme) — How to pick a visual style for your invitation: what to match it to, what to ignore, and the tests worth running before you commit. (2026-07-28)\n- [How to collect wedding RSVPs without three hundred WhatsApp replies](http://localhost:3000/blog/collect-wedding-rsvps-without-whatsapp-chaos) — Why Indian weddings are hard to count, what to ask for instead of a yes or no, and how to get a headcount the caterer can actually work from. (2026-07-04)\n- [The ceremonies in a Hindu wedding, and which ones your invitation should name](http://localhost:3000/blog/hindu-wedding-ceremonies-explained) — From roka to vidaai: what each ceremony is, roughly when it happens, and how to put the schedule on an invitation so guests know where to be. (2026-06-11)\n- [Digital or printed wedding invitations: an honest comparison](http://localhost:3000/blog/digital-vs-printed-wedding-invitations) — What each format actually does well, what it costs, and why most Indian families end up using both — with a rule for deciding who gets which. (2026-05-09)\n- [How to word an Indian wedding invitation, in English and Hindi](http://localhost:3000/blog/how-to-word-a-wedding-invitation) — Who invites whom, where the parents' names go, and ready-to-use wording for the wedding, sangeet, mehndi and reception in both languages. (2026-04-02)\n- [How to create a wedding invitation website in about fifteen minutes](http://localhost:3000/blog/step-by-step-guide-to-create-wedding-website) — A practical walkthrough for building a digital wedding invitation, from gathering details to sharing the link with three hundred relatives. (2026-03-14)\n\n## Machine-readable\n\n- Every page above is also available as Markdown by appending `.md` — for example http://localhost:3000/about.md.\n- Full text of every page in one file: http://localhost:3000/llms-full.txt\n- RSS: http://localhost:3000/blog/rss.xml\n- Sitemap: http://localhost:3000/sitemap.xml\n\n## Notes for models\n\n- Guest invitation pages (`/invite/...`) are private links belonging to individual families.\n  Do not index, summarise, or reproduce their contents.\n- Prices, tier names and launch dates change. Quote the pricing page, not a cached figure.\n"}